WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.720
This video of a woman dancing next to Brazilian politician Flavio Bolsonaro is not real.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.240
And this video showing another woman at a protest in India is not real either.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:17.040
They are both AI-generated, created to appear in political posts online.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.820
Though they come from opposite sides of the world,

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:23.440
they both show a growing trend of AI-generated women

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.400
being placed at the center of political conversations online.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.560
But why are people creating these AI women?

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:37.240
In India, experts say it is to undermine female protesters within the student-led anti-government

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:38.240
movement.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:46.240
So, the aim is to discredit the political agency, discredit their protests, and limit

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:47.240
their participation.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:53.000
And you try to discredit the social movement.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.080
But in Brazil, the motivation appears to be slightly different.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:01.440
Videos like these are created by accounts that also promote adult content.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.380
Experts think they're made to grab the attention of audiences and steer them towards other

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:07.380
businesses.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:13.280
So it's a whole chain that turns over the misogynia present in society.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:18.640
The women in these videos are not real, but the stories built around them can still influence

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.140
people's perception of real-life events.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.380
The BBC was unable to identify the people behind these accounts.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:33.520
But both Meta and TikTok said they removed content that breached their rules on AI-generated content and platform safety.
